---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/blog.css';

const title = "An agent can't be its own authority · CloakPipe";
const description = "Sandboxes limit where an agent can reach. An action boundary decides whether this exact tool call may run, from trusted inputs only, and signed evidence proves what happened.";
const MEDIUM = 'https://medium.com/@vidhiwaghela./your-ai-agent-finished-the-task-can-your-system-prove-what-happened-2a9015cb1e39';
---
<Base title={title} description={description} page="blog" ogType="article">
  <article class="blog">
    <div class="eyebrow"><a href="/blog">Blog</a></div>
    <h1>An agent can't be its own authority</h1>
    <p class="dek">Sandboxes limit where an agent can reach. Something outside the model has to decide whether this exact call may run, and something outside the operator has to prove what happened when it did.</p>
    <div class="byline"><time datetime="2026-10-11">11 Oct 2026</time> · CloakPipe</div>

    <p>
      Vidhi Waghela's article
      <a href={MEDIUM}>"Your AI agent finished the task. Can your system prove what happened?"</a>
      asks a question most agent stacks cannot answer today. It is worth reading in full, and it prompted this
      post. Here is how we think about the answer in CloakPipe, and which parts of it we are building into the
      open-source core.
    </p>

    <h2>Where a sandbox stops</h2>
    <p>
      The usual first step for running an agent safely is containment. Put it in a container, restrict the
      network, hand it scoped credentials. That work is necessary, and it answers one question well: which
      systems can this process reach at all?
    </p>
    <p>
      It does not answer the next question. Inside the sandbox, an agent holding a CRM token can patch any
      record that token can patch. The credential is scoped to the agent, not to the task the agent was given
      a minute ago. When the model proposes <code>crm.patch</code> on another customer's record, the sandbox
      lets it through, because the CRM is within reach and the token is valid.
    </p>
    <p>
      The model chooses what to call from its context, and that context holds text from users, retrieved
      documents and earlier tool results. Any of those can steer it. If the model's own proposal is what
      authorizes the call, then the real authority is whoever last wrote into its context. That is the sense in
      which an agent cannot be its own authority.
    </p>

    <h2>A boundary decides the exact call</h2>
    <p>
      An action boundary sits between the model and the tool. It treats every tool call as a proposal and
      decides on it using only trusted inputs: the certified release the agent runs as, a contract for each
      tool, the task context written by the orchestrator, and signed approvals. Nothing the agent sends can
      change those inputs, including arguments and request metadata. To decide well, the boundary needs four
      things.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Argument rules scoped to the task.</strong> A contract lists the arguments each tool accepts and
        the values they may take, and binds fields like the tenant and record to the current task. An argument
        the contract does not declare is refused. A refusal names the rule that failed, never the value that
        failed it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Approval bound to the exact payload.</strong> An approval that says "the agent may update the
        CRM" approves a category. A useful approval covers one call: the approver signs a fingerprint of the
        release, the tool and the full arguments, for one task, with an expiry. Change a single character of
        the arguments and the approval no longer matches anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A stable identity for each operation.</strong> Every side-effecting call gets an operation ID
        derived from the task and the exact call, or taken from its approval. The model never chooses it. When
        the same call arrives again, the boundary knows it has seen it before, and a completed call is answered
        from its stored result instead of running twice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Holding uncertain outcomes.</strong> This is the hard case. The call went out and the tool
        server died before answering. Did the refund go through? A blind retry may pay twice. Dropping it may
        not pay at all. The boundary records the operation as unknown and refuses every retry until an operator
        checks the real system and records what happened, done or not applied.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of this needs the model to behave. The model can propose anything it likes. What runs is decided
      elsewhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Test both directions</h2>
    <p>
      There is an easy way to pass every authorization test: refuse everything. A boundary that blocks the
      attack and also blocks the customer's legitimate refund has not done its job; it has moved the failure
      somewhere less visible.
    </p>
    <p>
      So we certify the boundary in both directions. Each test case is labelled with what should happen, allow
      or deny. A release must block the cases it should block and complete the authorized work it is meant to
      do. Under-blocking and over-refusal are separate failures with separate reasons, and a suite that is
      missing either kind of case cannot pass at all.
    </p>

    <h2>Proof that does not depend on the operator</h2>
    <p>
      A decision is only half the story. Afterwards, someone will ask what happened: a customer, an auditor, an
      incident review. A log written by the same system it describes is a claim, not proof, because whoever
      runs that system can edit it.
    </p>
    <p>
      CloakPipe records each decision as a hop in a hash-chained, signed evidence ledger, bound to the hash of
      the certified release that made it. An approved call carries the digest of its approval, so an auditor
      can match the operator's receipt to the ledger entry. Reconciling an uncertain operation is recorded as
      its own entry, so "we checked and it went through" is evidence too. The ledger holds no raw argument
      values, and not even plain hashes of them, since short values like phone numbers can be recovered by
      brute force.
    </p>
    <p>
      An exported bundle can be anchored at a public timestamp authority and a transparency log, so the operator
      cannot back-date it. An auditor then verifies it offline with published public keys, with no access to
      the operator's systems and no need to trust us.
    </p>

    <h2>Where this stands</h2>
    <p>
      The release gate, which refuses tool calls from an agent that is not running a certified release, and the
      evidence ledger are on <code>main</code> of the open-source core. Tool contracts, signed approvals,
      operation tracking and two-way boundary certification are in progress there now. The
      <a href="/docs/mcp-gate#action-boundary">MCP tool gate docs</a> describe the interface we are building,
      and the <a href="/docs/certification#boundary-rule">certification docs</a> describe the boundary rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are limits worth stating plainly. MCP has no standard way to ask a server whether a call took
      effect, so reconciliation is a manual step for now. The boundary only sees calls that pass through it.
      And a contract is only as good as the rules written into it: the boundary enforces them exactly, but it
      cannot tell you that you forgot one.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Thanks to Vidhi Waghela, whose <a href={MEDIUM}>article on proving what an agent did</a> prompted this
        post. CloakPipe is open source; the code is on <a href="https://github.com/rohansx/cloakpipe">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
